; Test: Verify Exact Execution Order of Fractal Priorities
;
; This test demonstrates CLEARLY that tuple priorities enable
; infinite subdivision (Sierpiński triangle pattern)

; ============================================================================
; SETUP: Create markers that fire in fractal order
; ============================================================================

(trigger)

; Coarsest level: (1), (2), (3)
(exec (1)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1)))))

(exec (2)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (2)))))

(exec (3)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (3)))))

; First subdivision: insert (1 5) and (2 5)
(exec (1 5)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1 5)))))

(exec (2 5)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (2 5)))))

; Second subdivision: insert (1 2), (1 7), (2 2), (2 7)
(exec (1 2)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1 2)))))

(exec (1 7)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1 7)))))

(exec (2 2)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (2 2)))))

(exec (2 7)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (2 7)))))

; Third subdivision: (1 5 2), (1 5 7), etc.
(exec (1 5 2)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1 5 2)))))

(exec (1 5 7)
  (, (trigger))
  (O (+ (fired-at-priority (1 5 7)))))

; ============================================================================
; EXPECTED EXECUTION ORDER (reading the Sierpiński triangle left-to-right):
; ============================================================================
;
; (1)
; (1 2)
; (1 5)
; (1 5 2)
; (1 5 7)
; (1 7)
; (2)
; (2 2)
; (2 5)
; (2 7)
; (3)
;
; This creates a fractal structure:
;
;        (1) ──────────── (2) ──────────── (3)
;        │                │
;    ┌───┴───┐        ┌───┴───┐
;  (1 2)  (1 5)     (2 2)  (2 5)
;         │  │              │
;      ┌──┴─┬┴──┐        ┌──┘
;   (1 5 2)(1 5 7)
;
; You can ALWAYS insert a new priority between any two existing ones!
; This is the Sierpiński triangle property.

; ============================================================================
; ALGORITHM SUMMARY
; ============================================================================
;
; To insert between priority A and priority B:
;
; 1. Same length:
;    between (2 5) and (2 7) → insert (2 6)
;    (midpoint of last element)
;
; 2. B extends A:
;    between (2) and (2 5) → insert (2 2)
;    (half of B's extension)
;
; 3. Same prefix, different suffix:
;    between (1 5) and (1 7) → insert (1 6)
;    (midpoint at first difference)
;
; 4. B extends A with multiple elements:
;    between (1 5) and (1 5 7) → insert (1 5 3)
;    (half of first extension element)
;
; This allows INFINITE refinement!
;
; Compare to lexicographic (like decimals):
; - between 1 and 2 → 1.5
; - between 1 and 1.5 → 1.25
; - between 1 and 1.25 → 1.125
; - etc.
;
; With tuples:
; - between (1) and (2) → (1 5)
; - between (1) and (1 5) → (1 2)
; - between (1) and (1 2) → (1 1)
; - etc.
;
; The SAME infinite subdivision property! ✅
